---
// /publish: what publishing is, before anyone signs in. Three ways in, what happens after the tag, and what the
// reviewer checks. The wizard itself is /import.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { cacheFor } from "../lib/page";

cacheFor(Astro, 3600);
const steps = [
  { t: "Validate", d: "Within a minute of the tag: the manifest, the files it names, and that the image or wheel exists. The tagged commit gets an otinstaller/publish status." },
  { t: "Scan", d: "Tools: signature, vulnerabilities, secrets, malware heuristics, an SBOM and a smoke test with your test input, offline. Guides: links and personal-data checks." },
  { t: "Review", d: "A new publisher's first release only: a person checks it against the acceptable-use rules, usually within 3 business days. Later releases skip this." },
  { t: "Publish", d: "The status turns green, you get an email, and ot install works. The registry copies the artifact and a bundle of the repository as a backup." },
];
const checklist = [
  "The tool fits the acceptable-use rules (lawful investigation, due diligence, threat intelligence, journalism, research).",
  "The README says what data the tool touches and the ethics answers match it (contacts the target, logs in, collects personal data).",
  "The licence allows redistribution and upstream work is credited.",
  "The namespace does not impersonate a person or project, and the account is not obviously throwaway.",
];
---
<Base title="Publish a tool or guide" description="Publish an OSINT tool or guide from the repository you already have: import it, add a manifest, or write a guide; push a tag to release." jsonHref="https://api.otinstaller.com/v1/tools?kind=tool&limit=1">
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Publish</p>
    <h1>From the repository you already have</h1>
    <p class="muted">You publish by pushing a <code>vX.Y.Z</code> tag. There is no upload: the registry reads your GitHub or GitLab repository, checks the release, and lists it. Pick the way in that fits.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="ways">
    <section class="way">
      <p class="eyebrow">Have a repository</p>
      <h2>Import it</h2>
      <p class="muted">The wizard detects a Docker tool, a Python package or a guide, drafts <code>otinstaller.yaml</code>, asks you only for what it cannot guess, and opens a pull or merge request. Merge, run the release workflow, done.</p>
      <p><a class="btn btn-strong" href="/import">Start the import wizard →</a></p>
      <p class="small muted">Or from the terminal: <code>ot import https://github.com/you/tool</code></p>
    </section>
    <section class="way">
      <p class="eyebrow">Prefer to write it</p>
      <h2>Add a manifest yourself</h2>
      <p class="muted"><code>ot init</code> writes the manifest, a README if you have none, and the release workflow. Fill in inputs, outputs, the command and the three ethics answers; <code>ot validate</code> checks it as the registry will.</p>
      <p><a class="btn" href="/docs/publishing">How publishing works →</a> <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/docs/manifest">Manifest reference</a></p>
    </section>
    <section class="way">
      <p class="eyebrow">Teach a method</p>
      <h2>Write a guide</h2>
      <p class="muted">A guide is Markdown pages, versioned like a tool, with the tools it uses linked and installable in one command. <code>ot init --guide</code> scaffolds it; <code>ot release 1.0.0</code> publishes it without CI.</p>
      <p><a class="btn" href="/docs/guides">Writing guides →</a></p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <section class="after" aria-labelledby="after-h">
    <div class="section-head"><h2 id="after-h">What happens after the tag</h2><a href="/docs/security">Security model →</a></div>
    <ol class="timeline">
      {steps.map((s, i) => <li><span class="n">0{i + 1}</span><div><h3>{s.t}</h3><p class="small muted">{s.d}</p></div></li>)}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <div class="two">
    <section aria-labelledby="rev-h">
      <h2 id="rev-h">What the reviewer checks</h2>
      <ul class="checks small">{checklist.map((c) => <li>{c}</li>)}</ul>
      <p class="small muted">Outcomes: approved, changes requested with what to fix, or rejected with one appeal by email. The same rules apply to every later release automatically.</p>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="keep-h">
      <h2 id="keep-h">Keeping it healthy</h2>
      <ul class="checks small">
        <li>Every night the registry runs your test input again; three failures in a row mark the tool possibly broken until one passes.</li>
        <li>New tags are picked up by webhook, and by a poll every six hours as a backstop. Your dashboard shows each release and why any failed.</li>
        <li>Yank a version to take it off <code>@latest</code>; deprecate a package to point people at a replacement; transfer it to another namespace when it changes hands.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="small muted">A manifest can also be held by the registry when a repository cannot carry one; it is labelled and unsigned, and a one-click pull request moves it into the repository later.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-head { margin-bottom: 24px; }
  .page-head .eyebrow { margin: 0; }
  .page-head h1 { margin: 6px 0; }
  .page-head p { margin: 0; max-width: 72ch; }
  .ways { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1px; background: var(--border); border: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .way { background: var(--bg); padding: 20px; display: grid; gap: 8px; align-content: start; }
  .way .eyebrow, .way p { margin: 0; }
  .way h2 { margin: 0; font-size: var(--fs-4); }
  .after { margin-top: 40px; }
  .timeline { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1px; background: var(--border); border: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .timeline li { background: var(--bg); padding: 16px; display: grid; gap: 8px; align-content: start; }
  .timeline .n { font-size: var(--fs-0); color: var(--text-faint); letter-spacing: 0.12em; }
  .timeline h3 { margin: 0; font-size: var(--fs-3); }
  .timeline p { margin: 2px 0 0; }
  .two { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 32px; margin-top: 40px; }
  .two h2 { font-size: var(--fs-4); margin: 0 0 10px; }
  .checks { margin: 0; padding-left: 18px; display: grid; gap: 6px; }
  .two p { margin: 12px 0 0; }
  @media (max-width: 860px) { .ways, .timeline { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .two { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 24px; } }
</style>
